import {icon} from './app.js?v=8';
export function teacher(){return `<div class="page-heading"><div><h1>Teacher's corner</h1><p>EDUP3133 · Task 3 · Year 4 Science · Unit 4: Photosynthesis</p></div><a href="offline.html" class="btn secondary">${icon('download')} Printable trail</a></div><div class="notice"><strong>Before classroom use and submission</strong><p>Complete usability testing with at least two adults, record the changes, confirm lecturer approval for a custom website, and add your official cover details to the private submission. Do not publish identification numbers here.</p></div><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Learning intentions and success criteria</h2><p>Pupils aged about 10, with prior knowledge that plants are living things and need water. English instruction; use partner talk and teacher explanation when needed.</p><ol><li>Explain photosynthesis as the process by which green plants make food.</li><li>Name sunlight, water, carbon dioxide and chlorophyll; identify starch (stored food) and oxygen. Explain the textbook link: glucose can be stored as starch.</li><li>Make and test a prediction by changing one model condition.</li><li>Give reasons why photosynthesis matters: food, oxygen for breathing, and maintaining the balance of gases in the air.</li><li>Correct an inaccurate AI claim using two sources and remove private information before sharing.</li></ol><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> prediction, experiment log, two connected creek paths, evidence repair and privacy task, Nico’s unscored forest adventure and a written or spoken reflection.</p><p class="caption">Content alignment: supplied KSSR Science Year 4 DSKP, printed page 54 (PDF page 66), learning standards 4.2.1–4.2.4. Textbook: printed pages 76–81, with a question-card game on page 82. This site focuses on photosynthesis, not the entire Year 4 curriculum. Creative communication standard 4.2.5 is outside this assessment’s targets.</p></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>A flexible lesson sequence</h2><p><strong>Instructional design:</strong> Gagné's Nine Events of Instruction. <strong>Technology integration:</strong> TPACK, integrating accurate science, guided enquiry and interactive media.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>RPH phase / time</th><th>Pupil experience</th><th>Gagné mapping and evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Induction · 2 min</td><td>Plant mystery; predict where food comes from.</td><td>Gain attention, state outcomes, recall prior knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>Explanation · 3 min</td><td>Choose the cartoon and guided playable leaf factory, or the original picture notes. Watch first, then send water, admit carbon dioxide and uncover sunlight.</td><td>Present content and guide learning.</td></tr><tr><td>Exploration · 3 min</td><td>Predict, manipulate one variable, compare model trials.</td><td>Elicit performance; give explanatory feedback.</td></tr><tr><td>Application · 3 min</td><td>Rotate channels and solve two water-routing puzzles.</td><td>Practise applying the water-to-root relationship.</td></tr><tr><td>AI investigation · 3 min</td><td>Inspect sources, repair a claim, redact private data.</td><td>Apply learning and receive feedback.</td></tr><tr><td>Assessment · flexible</td><td>Explore one continuous 3D forest. Move branches, guide water and light, inspect a leaf, and help Sprout and the woodland recover. No timer or marks.</td><td>Assess performance without a timer.</td></tr><tr><td>Closure · 2 min</td><td>Three discoveries, reflection and outdoor transfer prompt.</td><td>Support retention and transfer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="caption">The two explanation modes are alternatives within the same 3-minute stop, not two compulsory activities. Timing is a design estimate, not a measured result. Flexible navigation supports revision; station completion records participation, not a formal mastery grade.</p></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>DSKP alignment: photosynthesis</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Learning standard</th><th>Evidence on this site</th><th>Forest evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4.2.1 Meaning</td><td>Plant mystery; cartoon, leaf model and picture notes.</td><td>Explain how green plants make their own food.</td></tr><tr><td>4.2.2 Needs</td><td>Leaf demonstration, four-condition experiment and water-to-roots game.</td><td>Uncover seedlings, bring water to roots and inspect what the green leaf needs.</td></tr><tr><td>4.2.3 Products observed through media</td><td>Leaf model shows stored food and released oxygen; product clues connect glucose to starch.</td><td>Observe the leaf model making food and releasing oxygen.</td></tr><tr><td>4.2.4 Importance, with reasoning</td><td>Food-chain and gas-exchange explanations; garden scenarios.</td><td>Help the woodland pantry and breathing pond recover; discuss food and oxygen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The AI detective is an additional literacy activity, not a photosynthesis learning standard. The creek game practises water delivery; it does not investigate plant responses to stimuli. The condition model is a simulation, not a real plant investigation. Adventure completion and participation badges do not establish official Performance Levels; teacher observation and appropriate investigations are needed for those claims.</p><p><strong>Assessment:</strong> Observe pupils’ predictions, explanations and changes to the forest. The campaign gives contextual feedback and lets pupils try again. Its completion certificate records participation, without a mark or official DSKP performance level.</p></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Two KPM AI-literacy domains</h2><p><strong>Pengajaran dan Pembelajaran (Teaching and Learning):</strong> pupils critically check a teacher-selected simulated AI response, consult two sources and repair a misconception.</p><p><strong>Etika AI (AI Ethics):</strong> pupils practise removing personal data, acknowledging AI assistance and crediting sources. The teacher remains responsible for checking content.</p><p>The brief allows simulated or teacher-selected AI output; pupils do not need a live AI account. Gamification is the contemporary technology used in the learning experience.</p><a href="https://www.moe.gov.my/surat-pekeliling-ikhtisas-kpm-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KPM AI Literacy Guide and SPI Bil. 2 Tahun 2026 ↗</a></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Media, inclusion and safe use</h2><ul><li><strong>CASPER:</strong> contrast for text and controls, alignment, simple instructions, proximity of labels to diagrams, selective emphasis and repeated navigation patterns.</li><li><strong>Multimedia:</strong> original illustration, text, controlled animation, optional speech and generated sound cues. Segmenting, signalling, coherence and spatial contiguity guide presentation.</li><li><strong>Access:</strong> keyboard/touch controls, reduced motion, larger text, low-data setting, transcript, printable alternative and no timed failure. Audio depends on browser voice availability.</li><li><strong>Privacy:</strong> no accounts, analytics, uploads or live AI requests in the app. Progress and reflection are device-local; explain shared-device clearing.</li><li><strong>Differentiation:</strong> read aloud, use paired talk, offer one-condition experiments; extend by explaining why a fair test changes one variable.</li></ul></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Submission evidence</h2><p>The private project folder includes a seven-page documentation draft, source backup, an AI and asset log, and an adult usability-test form. Two genuine adult tests and their resulting improvements must be added before submission.</p><p>Greenhaven is available from the welcome page and the assessment stop. The current forest campaign is unscored. Pupils manipulate the environment, receive feedback and can revisit each clearing. Separate device-local explorer profiles keep progress apart. Completing the forest unlocks a named PDF certificate without marks; it is not a formal DSKP performance level.</p><a href="#credits">Sources, attribution and safety guidance</a></section>`}
export function credits(){return `<div class="page-heading"><div><h1>Explore kindly. Check carefully.</h1><p>Good scientists look after people as well as plants.</p></div></div><section class="safety-list"><div>${icon('shield')}<h2>Keep private things private.</h2><p>Never share your password, full name, address or a friend's photo with an AI tool. Ask an adult before using a new service.</p></div><div>${icon('search')}<h2>Check before you believe.</h2><p>Compare a claim with a science textbook or a named science organisation. An attractive picture or confident answer can still be inaccurate.</p></div><div>${icon('book')}<h2>Give credit.</h2><p>Say where ideas and pictures came from. Tell your teacher if AI helped. Do not pretend someone else's work is your own.</p></div></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Where our science comes from</h2><ul class="source-list"><li><strong><em>Science Year 4</em>, Unit 4.</strong> User-supplied KSSR textbook, printed pages 76–81. Primary science content; ideas paraphrased and diagrams newly made. The full textbook is not redistributed.</li><li><strong>KSSR Science Year 4 DSKP, Semakan 2017.</strong> Supplied curriculum document, printed page 54: 4.2 Photosynthesis, standards 4.2.1–4.2.4. The document is Year 4; the earlier Year 2 note was incorrect.</li><li><strong>NASA Science. (n.d.). <em>What is the carbon cycle?</em></strong> Independent science cross-check. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/what-is-the-carbon-cycle/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read with an adult ↗</a></li><li><strong>Royal Horticultural Society. Plant science references.</strong> Water moves through roots and internal vessels; leaf pores exchange gases, and chlorophyll absorbs light. <a href="https://www.rhs.org.uk/advice/understanding-plants/how-plants-absorb-water" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Water transport</a> · <a href="https://www.rhs.org.uk/advice/understanding-plants/how-plants-breathe" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gas exchange</a> · <a href="https://www.rhs.org.uk/advice/understanding-plants/how-plants-use-light-to-grow" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chlorophyll and light</a></li><li><strong>Kementerian Pendidikan Malaysia. (2026). <em>Panduan Literasi Kecerdasan Buatan (AI).</em></strong> <a href="https://www.moe.gov.my/surat-pekeliling-ikhtisas-kpm-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official guide and circular ↗</a></li><li><strong>NASA. (n.d.). <em>STEMonstrations: Photosynthesis.</em></strong> Optional teacher-led extension video; aimed at older pupils, outside the core lesson. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/stem-content/stemonstrations-photosynthesis/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit NASA's video page ↗</a></li></ul></section><section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>How this learning space was made</h2><p>Code, activity drafts and original garden and character artwork were created with assistance from OpenAI Codex and image generation. The storybook characters and garden scenes use original AI-assisted illustrations. The plant diagrams use interactive SVG geometry. Science was checked against the supplied textbook and the linked NASA and RHS sources. Final teacher review and genuine adult usability testing are still required.</p><p>The AI claim in the detective activity is an intentionally incorrect, authored simulation. There is no live chatbot. The garden character is fictional; its face is decorative.</p><p>Typefaces: <strong>Nunito</strong>, <strong>Lilita One</strong> and <strong>Caveat</strong>, under the SIL Open Font License (<a href="assets/OFL-Nunito.txt">Nunito</a>, <a href="assets/OFL-LilitaOne.txt">Lilita One</a>, <a href="assets/OFL-Caveat.txt">Caveat</a>). Icons and instructional diagrams are original SVG geometry. Sound cues are generated by the browser; read-aloud uses available device voices.</p><p><strong>Greenhaven 3D assets:</strong> nature models are from Quaternius’s Stylized Nature MegaKit Standard (CC0; <a href="assets/grove/nature-license.txt">included license</a>). Nico is a supplied third-party character; its original creator and license were not identified in the supplied folder. The procedural Sprout model, creek gate and scene code were created with Codex assistance. Downloaded models are not claimed as the student’s original artwork. Three.js is used under its <a href="vendor/three/LICENSE.txt">MIT license</a>. This scene stores an optional explorer nickname locally and sends no pupil answers to a server.</p><p>External links open another website, which has its own privacy terms. No external video is loaded automatically.</p></section><a class="btn" href="#map">${icon('map')} Back to my adventure</a>`}
